#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.84pt, 791.4pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 14.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 69.96pt, "1.")
  #line-at(103.079pt, 69.96pt, "Introduction ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 91.68pt, "The global capacity of Variable Renewable Energy (VRE), including solar and wind, is 3 TW in ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 110.636pt, "2024 [1] and is projected to reach 30 TW within the next 25 years [2], at which point it would ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 129.591pt, "represent more than 80% of total world electricity generation capacity. However, the inherent ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 148.547pt, "intermittency of these sources and the temporal mismatch between generation and demand ")
  #line-at(85.047pt, 167.503pt, "necessitate effective balancing measures. Sector coupling, particularly through heat ")
  #line-at(85.036pt, 186.458pt, "electrification, has been identified as a cost-effective pathway for integrating VRE and ")
  #line-at(85.047pt, 205.414pt, "decarbonize the energy sector [3], [4]. Yet, while the additional power loads induced by heat ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 224.37pt, "electrification can provide flexibility that initially reduces storage requirements, energy storage ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 243.447pt, "systems remain essential to cost-effectively balance fluctuations in solar and wind generation as ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 262.403pt, "renewable penetration increases [2].  ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 293.359pt, "Although Pumped Hydro Storage (PHS) currently dominate global energy storage installed ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 312.315pt, "capacity [5], [6] falling costs of lithium-ion (Li-ion) batteries have accelerated their deployment, ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 331.27pt, "accounting for 80 % of newly installed battery capacity in 2023 [6]. The techno-economic ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 350.226pt, "characteristics of Li-ion batteries – characterized by a high cost per energy (CPE) capacity, a ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 369.182pt, "relatively low cost per power (CPP) capacity and a very high round-trip efficiency – make them ")
  #line-at(85.102pt, 388.137pt, "particularly well suited for addressing short-duration or intra-day fluctuations [4], [7], [8], ")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 407.093pt, "especially in solar-dominated regions. Nevertheless, achieving a high share of VRE cost-")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 426.17pt, "effectively will require complementing short-duration solutions with long-duration energy ")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 445.126pt, "storage (LDES) [7]. LDES technologies are distinguished by their low CPE, making them crucial ")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 464.081pt, "for addressing extended periods of low renewable generation. ")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 495.038pt, "A notable example of LDES technology is Power-to-Heat-to-Power Storage (PHPS), also known ")
  #line-at(85.113pt, 513.993pt, "as Carnot battery [9] or Electro Thermal Energy Storage (ETES) [10], among others. In PHPS ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 532.949pt, "systems, electricity is converted into heat via a Power-to-Heat (P2H) converter and stored as either ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 551.905pt, "sensible [11] or latent [12] heat at high temperatures. The stored heat is then converted back into ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 570.86pt, "electricity using a Heat-to-Power (H2P) converter.  ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 601.817pt, "This study is focused on PHPS systems that use electrical resistance heating to convert electricity ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 620.894pt, "into heat [13], [14], [15], and therefore does not consider the potential use of heat pumps as H2P ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 639.849pt, "unit [16]. For thermal storage, low-cost materials such as ferrosilicon alloys [15], graphite blocks ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 658.805pt, "[17], [18], other ceramics ([19]), clay bricks [20], or stones ([21]), have been used, insulated from ")
  #line-at(85.047pt, 677.761pt, "the environment with inexpensive porous refractory materials. The combination of low material ")
  #line-at(85.047pt, 696.716pt, "cost (in €/kg) and high energy density (in Wh/kg) yields a low CPE (in €/Wh), a key requirement ")
  #line-at(85.035pt, 715.672pt, "for LDES applications [15]. The H2P converter is a critical component of the system, as it largely ")
  #line-at(85.024pt, 734.628pt, "determines the CPP and contributes most significantly to the round-trip conversion efficiency. ")
  #line-at(85.024pt, 753.583pt, "Energy losses from this conversion, released as low-temperature heat, can be recovered; and ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 4pt), 103.5pt, 603.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.84pt, 791.4pt, "3 ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 66.955pt, "similar to a Combined Heat and Power (CHP) plant, PHPS can deliver both heat and electricity ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 86.032pt, "in their output. ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 116.989pt, "Companies like 1414 Degrees [22], E2S Power [18], Rondo [23] or Gamesa [21] have suggested ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 135.944pt, "a storage technology combined with well-established turbogenerators (e.g., steam turbines) as ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 154.9pt, "H2P converter. Alternatively, other companies like Antora [14], the Fourth Power [24], or ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 173.856pt, "Thermophoton [25], are developing solid-state alternatives based on Thermophotovoltaics (TPV) ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 192.811pt, "[26], [27]. ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 223.767pt, "Previous techno-economic analyses of PHPS systems, charged via resistive heating, have ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 242.723pt, "primarily focused on their feasibility when included in residential settings. These studies often ")
  #line-at(85.047pt, 261.8pt, "examine applications such as reducing natural gas consumption [28], [29] or their role in fully ")
  #line-at(85.025pt, 280.756pt, "electrified buildings [15], [30], frequently comparing PHPS with Li-ion batteries. Peng et al. [11] ")
  #line-at(85.003pt, 299.712pt, "studied the use of a graphite based PHPS system with TPV for energy discharge in the steel and ")
  #line-at(85.003pt, 318.667pt, "iron industry, highlighting its potential for long duration energy storage. Other research has ")
  #line-at(85.003pt, 337.623pt, "looked into hybridizing PHPS with Li-ion batteries to boost the synergies of both storage ")
  #line-at(85.003pt, 356.579pt, "technologies [30]. Other works have also analysed the impact of leveraging waste heat from H2P ")
  #line-at(85.003pt, 375.534pt, "conversion in large building applications [31]. On a broader scale, several works have assessed ")
  #line-at(84.992pt, 394.49pt, "the feasibility of PHPS systems storing energy in molten salts, comparing their performance to ")
  #line-at(84.98pt, 413.446pt, "other configurations including Li-ion batteries, wind turbines or PV installations [10], [32]. Most ")
  #line-at(84.98pt, 432.523pt, "of the aforementioned works rely on techno-economic models that optimize the system sizing, ")
  #line-at(84.969pt, 451.479pt, "with the exception of those by Okazaki [10] and Peng et al. [11]. ")
  #line-at(84.98pt, 482.435pt, "Prior assessments of PHPS economic feasibility assumed that its integration has a negligible ")
  #line-at(84.958pt, 501.39pt, "impact on electricity prices and the overall energy system configuration, thereby limiting their ")
  #line-at(84.958pt, 520.346pt, "relevance for system-level analysis. In contrast, this study integrates PHPS into a stylized, ")
  #line-at(84.98pt, 539.302pt, "country-wise energy system model in Europe. Our model includes wind- and solar-dominated ")
  #line-at(84.991pt, 558.258pt, "systems, sector coupling with heating, and evaluates the impact of waste heat utilization. Within ")
  #line-at(84.991pt, 577.213pt, "this comprehensive framework, this analysis addresses the following research questions: ")
  #line-at(121.004pt, 608.898pt, "What is the optimal capacity and operation of PHPS under different scenarios?")
  #line-at(121.004pt, 628.693pt, "How do varying technical and economic assumptions influence these outcomes?  ")
  #line-at(85.002pt, 659.77pt, "For the low-temperature heating sector (i.e., domestic hot water), we assume complete ")
  #line-at(85.013pt, 678.726pt, "electrification through heat pumps and heat resistors. PHPS is analysed in combination with two ")
  #line-at(85.024pt, 697.682pt, "types of low-temperature thermal storage systems: centralized thermal energy storage (CTES), ")
  #line-at(85.024pt, 716.637pt, "often installed in district heating systems in Baltic and Scandinavian countries, and to a lesser ")
  #line-at(85.013pt, 735.593pt, "extent, some Eastern and South-Eastern European regions [35], [36], [37]; and individual thermal ")
  #line-at(85.024pt, 754.549pt, "energy storage (ITES), which is more typical in decentralized heating systems in Southern ")
]

