#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(294.84pt, 791.4pt, "8 ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 14.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 69.96pt, "3.")
  #line-at(103.079pt, 69.96pt, "Results ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 91.68pt, "Figure 3 contains the optimal Li-ion, PHPS and CTES or ITES energy storage capacity for the ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 110.636pt, "different configurations as a percentage of the total energy capacity within the optimal system. ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 129.591pt, "We compared results for energy systems of wind (panels a and b) and solar dominated regions ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 148.547pt, "(panels c and d) including (panels b and d) or not (panels a and c) the possibility of using the ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 167.503pt, "waste heat from the H2P conversion from PHPS. Optimal energy capacity, expressed in av.h.l, ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 186.458pt, "for the PHPS is included in Table 2. Optimal energy capacities of rest of the energy storage ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 205.414pt, "systems (Li-ion battery, CTES and ITES) are shown in Table B. 1. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 237.24pt, "3.1.")
  #line-at(120.48pt, 237.24pt, "PHPS selected for individual heating systems ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(85.08pt, 269.16pt, "We begin with the decentralized configuration, where PHPS proves to be a viable energy storage ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 288.116pt, "solution, regardless of the location and the use of waste heat. Its role is particularly pronounced ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 307.071pt, "in wind-dominated regions, as reflected in the higher investment in PHPS energy capacity shown ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 326.027pt, "in Table 2. This outcome is driven by the relatively low cost per unit of energy capacity, which ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 344.983pt, "makes PHPS well suited for long-duration storage that aligns with the low-frequency fluctuations ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 363.938pt, "characteristic of wind generation. ")
  #line-at(85.058pt, 395.016pt, "In wind-dominated regions, where heat demand is higher, the availability of ITES slightly reduces ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 413.972pt, "the optimal PHPS energy capacity, as ITES acts as a buffer between heat production and demand. ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 432.927pt, "This allows a greater share of surplus electricity to be converted directly into heat via heat pumps, ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 451.883pt, "a more efficient form of thermal energy storage. In solar-dominated regions, however, ITES has ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 470.839pt, "the opposite effect, slightly increasing the optimal PHPS capacity. Here, PHPS was previously ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 489.794pt, "constrained by the absence of a heat sink, which limited discharge. With ITES available, waste ")
  #line-at(85.069pt, 508.75pt, "heat can be stored, enabling greater utilization of PHPS and thus a larger optimal energy capacity. ")
]

