  #line-at(85.091pt, 104.988pt, "at higher power than it is discharged, making it capable of storing large amounts of energy for ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 123.944pt, "extended periods while releasing it more gradually. The possibility of decoupling charging and ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 142.899pt, "discharging power capacities, allows the system to invest in higher charging power and energy ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 161.855pt, "capacities, taking advantage of their relatively low cost, while reducing the discharging power, ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 180.811pt, "given its higher cost. As a result, the optimal PHPS configuration has a discharge duration at ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 199.766pt, "maximum discharging power capacity of ~42 h (panel d), well suited to balance wind fluctuations. ")
  #line-at(85.102pt, 218.722pt, "This is reflected in the synoptic behaviour of its normalized State of Charge (SOC) shown in panel ")
  #line-at(85.102pt, 237.799pt, "c (Figure 4) and in the charging and discharging patterns during the winter months illustrated in ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 256.755pt, "panel d. PHPS is also used to cover base electricity demand, seen by the constant and low PHPS ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 275.711pt, "discharge power in panels a and b. ")
  #line-at(85.091pt, 306.667pt, "Notably, panels b and d show that PHPS also balances the daily solar generation during the ")
