  #line-at(292.08pt, 791.4pt, "17 ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 66.955pt, "substantially more valuable, in some cases fully displacing lithium-ion batteries and lowering the ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 86.032pt, "system’s overall dependence on short-duration storage. In solar-dominated regions, this coupling ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 104.988pt, "even allows PHPS to coexist with CTES, although its capacity remains much smaller in ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 123.944pt, "comparison. Conversely, in wind-dominated regions with established district heating systems, ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 142.899pt, "CTES tends to outcompete PHPS, limiting its role to scenarios where decentralized heating or ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 161.855pt, "waste heat recovery make it competitive. ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 192.811pt, "Another notable finding is that PHPS can flexibly balance both synoptic-scale wind fluctuations ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 211.767pt, "and daily solar cycles, reflecting the advantage of decoupling its charging and discharging power ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 230.723pt, "capacities. However, its widespread deployment depends on achieving very low energy capacity ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 249.8pt, "costs—below 10 €/kWh—and maintaining reasonable heat-to-power conversion efficiencies. ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 268.755pt, "Cost reductions in lithium-ion batteries present an additional challenge, further narrowing the ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 287.711pt, "circumstances in which PHPS will be competitive. ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 318.667pt, "Overall, the study suggests that PHPS is most promising in solar-dominated countries with ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 337.623pt, "decentralized heating systems or large-scale buildings where space for centralized storage is ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 356.579pt, "limited. In these contexts, PHPS can simultaneously deliver electricity and heat, reduce reliance ")
  #line-at(85.08pt, 375.534pt, "on lithium-ion batteries, and contribute to system reliability. ")
